{
  "an10.14:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.14 ",
  "an10.14:0.2": "2. A Protector ",
  "an10.14:0.3": "Hard-heartedness ",
  "an10.14:1.1": "“Mendicants, a monk or nun who has not given up five kinds of hard-heartedness and has not severed five shackles of the heart can expect decline, not growth, in skillful qualities, whether by day or by night. ",
  "an10.14:2.1": "What are the five kinds of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up? ",
  "an10.14:2.2": "Firstly, a mendicant has doubts about the Teacher. They’re uncertain, undecided, and lacking confidence. ",
  "an10.14:2.3": "This being so, their mind doesn’t incline toward keenness, commitment, persistence, and striving. ",
  "an10.14:2.4": "This is the first kind of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up. ",
  "an10.14:3.1": "Furthermore, a mendicant has doubts about the teaching … ",
  "an10.14:3.2": "the Saṅgha … ",
  "an10.14:3.3": "the training … ",
  "an10.14:3.4": "A mendicant is angry and upset with their spiritual companions, resentful and closed off. ",
  "an10.14:3.5": "This being so, their mind doesn’t incline toward keenness, commitment, persistence, and striving. ",
  "an10.14:3.6": "This is the fifth kind of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up. ",
  "an10.14:3.7": "These are the five kinds of hard-heartedness they haven’t given up. ",
  "an10.14:4.1": "What are the five shackles of the heart they haven’t severed? ",
  "an10.14:4.2": "Firstly, a mendicant isn’t free of greed, desire, fondness, thirst, passion, and